Sval: a Quick Peek takes the click the user chose for it, in Firefox's place

Every click on a link with a modifier reaches ClickHandlerParent, which
opens it where BrowserUtils.whereToOpenLink says. The click that opens a
link in a Quick Peek can be one Firefox opens a tab or a window for, such as
Command-Option-click, so this asks SvalQuickPeek first and opens the link as
Firefox would only when no Quick Peek opened. A listener of Sval's own on
chromelinkclick cannot stop Firefox's: both listen to the same event, in
no order either can rely on. ClickHandlerChild also sends a page's own
scripted click while the page has the user's activation, so it says
whether the click is the user's, which a Quick Peek requires.

diff --git a/browser/actors/ClickHandlerChild.sys.mjs b/browser/actors/ClickHandlerChild.sys.mjs
index 3d8c409c7e..4469eeee80 100644
--- a/browser/actors/ClickHandlerChild.sys.mjs
+++ b/browser/actors/ClickHandlerChild.sys.mjs
@@ -112,6 +112,8 @@ export class ClickHandlerChild extends JSWindowActorChild {
       ctrlKey: event.ctrlKey,
       metaKey: event.metaKey,
       altKey: event.altKey,
+      // Sval opens a Quick Peek for the user's own click alone.
+      isTrusted: event.isTrusted,
       href: null,
       title: null,
       policyContainer,
